{
  "book": "MAT",
  "chapter": 20,
  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-66962133b319ae53": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-682",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Lightfoot (Hone Hebraicae) gives several citations from Jewish writers to show that the customary time of working was “from sun-rising to the appearing of the stars, and not from break of day.” The laborers, however, may have been hired before sunrise.",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-682",
          "sourceHash": "f7d98584e2b3863f1f8ab7b24ecacf417495e7e481d5d388a38c88f9a7427cee",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-a908d106acc774ec": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-686",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "See also Mark 10:37. It was evidently the intention of this ambitious mother to have positions of the greatest honor for her two sons. The right hand is usually considered the post of the highest honor. See 1 Kings 2:19; Psalm 45:9; 80:17. For this reason Jesus is said to be at t…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-686",
          "sourceHash": "b5f84a6a4be9c6427ba152854284adf3b12323cf219c634272eb52c016d7402a",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-bc161b812b494d77": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-683",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The denarius (penny) was the principal silver coin of the Romans. It was originally ten times the value of the as (see note on Matt. 10:29 #658) from which circumstance it derived its name. When, afterward, the weight of the as was reduced, the denarius was made equal to the weig…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-683",
          "sourceHash": "aff1f07172bc7d6aefae60f92cc3f782fe9affa48cb948e7b685273b902d7a86",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-dcb403f34aa28b7c": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-684",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The place for trading was often at the gates of walled cities. See note on 2 Kings 7:1 (#339). Here, also, laborers went to seek employment, and employers went to seek laborers. Sometimes, in Oriental cities, a large public square is used for similar purposes. Morier says: “The m…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-684",
          "sourceHash": "7c251212fda724cd6706d7d2d5f5522136aa4efa76e55ea0c2f916732b8dcf06",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-f4d81fa3d0db548f": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-685",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "This was according to the Mosaic law, which sought thus to protect the poor laborer from any employer who might wish to keep back his hire. “The wages of him that is hired shall not abide with thee all night until the morning” (Lev. 19:13). “Thou shalt not oppress a hired servant…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-685",
          "sourceHash": "0f2780e55aaac4a6568563efa383b041c7047ae67cbc53516845bfe00a30efe8",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      }
    },
